{
  "title": "{ip:Penso} che {csp:sia} una buona idea",
  "translation": "I think it's a good idea",
  "topics": "The congiuntivo presente: forms and when to use it",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "What the congiuntivo is for"
      },
      "The indicative states facts. The *congiuntivo* presents something through someone's mind: an opinion, a doubt, a feeling or a wish. It almost always appears after *che*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "The main verb expresses",
            "Examples",
            "Sentence"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Opinion",
              "{ip:penso} che, {ip:credo} che, mi {ip:sembra} che",
              "{ip:Penso} che {csp:sia} vero."
            ],
            [
              "Doubt",
              "{ip:dubito} che, non {ip:sono} sicuro che",
              "{ip:Dubito} che {csp:venga}."
            ],
            [
              "Feeling",
              "{ip:sono} contento che, {ip:ho} paura che, mi {ip:dispiace} che",
              "{ip:Sono} contento che tu {csp:stia} bene."
            ],
            [
              "Wish",
              "{ip:voglio} che, {ip:spero} che, {ip:preferisco} che",
              "{ip:Spero} che tu {csp:possa} venire."
            ],
            [
              "Impersonal judgement",
              "{ip:è} importante che, {ip:è} meglio che, {ip:bisogna} che",
              "{ip:È} meglio che tu {csp:parta}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "When the speaker is certain, use the indicative: *{ip:So} che {ip:è} vero. {ip:Sono} sicuro che {ip:viene}.*",
      {
        "h": "How to form it"
      },
      "The vowels swap: *-are* verbs take *-i*, while *-ere* and *-ire* verbs take *-a*. The three singular persons are identical, so you often need the pronoun: *che io {ip:parli}, che tu {csp:parli}*. The *noi* form is the same as in the present.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "",
            "parlare",
            "scrivere",
            "dormire",
            "capire"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "io, tu, lui / lei",
              "{csp:parl**i**}",
              "{csp:scriv**a**}",
              "{csp:dorm**a**}",
              "{csp:capisc**a**}"
            ],
            [
              "noi",
              "{csp:parl**iamo**}",
              "{csp:scriv**iamo**}",
              "{csp:dorm**iamo**}",
              "{csp:cap**iamo**}"
            ],
            [
              "voi",
              "{csp:parl**iate**}",
              "{csp:scriv**iate**}",
              "{csp:dorm**iate**}",
              "{csp:cap**iate**}"
            ],
            [
              "loro",
              "{csp:parl**ino**}",
              "{csp:scriv**ano**}",
              "{csp:dorm**ano**}",
              "{csp:capisc**ano**}"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "A shortcut for irregular verbs"
      },
      "Take the *io* form of the present and change the final *-o* to *-a*: *{ip:faccio} → {csp:faccia}, {ip:vengo} → {csp:venga}, {ip:dico} → {csp:dica}, {ip:esco} → {csp:esca}, {ip:bevo} → {csp:beva}, {ip:posso} → {csp:possa}, {ip:voglio} → {csp:voglia}*.",
      "Only a few verbs need learning by heart.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "",
            "essere",
            "avere",
            "andare",
            "stare",
            "dare"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "io, tu, lui / lei",
              "{csp:sia}",
              "{csp:abbia}",
              "{imv:vada}",
              "{csp:stia}",
              "{imv:dia}"
            ],
            [
              "noi",
              "{csp:siamo}",
              "{csp:abbiamo}",
              "{csp:andiamo}",
              "{csp:stiamo}",
              "{csp:diamo}"
            ],
            [
              "voi",
              "{csp:siate}",
              "{csp:abbiate}",
              "{csp:andiate}",
              "{csp:stiate}",
              "{csp:diate}"
            ],
            [
              "loro",
              "{csp:siano}",
              "{csp:abbiano}",
              "{csp:vadano}",
              "{csp:stiano}",
              "{csp:diano}"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Same person? Use the infinitive"
      },
      "If the two parts of the sentence have the same subject, Italian uses *di* with an infinitive instead: *{ip:Penso} di partire* (I think I'll leave), but *{ip:Penso} che tu {csp:parta}* (I think you are leaving). Verbs of wish take no *di*: *{ip:Voglio} partire*."
    ]
  },
